  "tasteProfileInfoTooltip": "About these taste dimensions",
  "tasteProfileInfoTitle": "Taste dimensions",
  "tasteProfileInfoIntro": "These five dimensions come from the Specialty Coffee Association (SCA) cupping vocabulary — the same framework professional cuppers use to describe coffee. They were chosen because they capture the structural taste of a brew (how it feels and tastes in the mouth) and stay meaningful regardless of origin or brew method, unlike specific flavor notes like 'blueberry' or 'cocoa'.",
  "tasteInfoSweetness": "How much pleasant sweetness comes through — caramel, honey, or fruit sugar. Higher sweetness usually indicates ripe, well-developed beans.",
  "tasteInfoBrightness": "The lively, refreshing acidity that makes a coffee feel crisp and clean — like the tang of an apple or citrus. Not the same as harsh sourness.",
  "tasteInfoBody": "The weight and texture of the coffee on your tongue — from light and tea-like to heavy and syrupy. Independent of strength or caffeine.",
  "tasteInfoBitterness": "The bitter element of the cup. Some bitterness adds depth and balance; too much usually points to over-extraction or dark roasting.",
  "tasteInfoAftertaste": "What lingers after you swallow — the length and character of the finish. A long, pleasant aftertaste is a hallmark of high-quality coffee.",
